ion across specified temperature regions and finishing stands.\n\nOverall, DEFORMATION is a central component of hot working and controlled rolling of steels. Its effectiveness in improving the steel’s mechanical and operational properties depends on the imposed plastic strain, deformation temperature, austenite recrystallization state, and whether processing occurs in the recrystallization, non-recrystallization, recovery, or two-phase regions.", "refusal": null, "role": "assistant", "annotations": null, "audio": null, "function_call": null, "tool_calls": null, "provider_specific_fields": {"refusal": null}}, "provider_specific_fields": {}}], "created": 1790704701, "model": "gpt-5.6-luna", "object": "chat.completion", "moderation": null, "service_tier": "default", "system_fingerprint": null, "usage": {"completion_tokens": 469, "prompt_tokens": 695, "total_tokens": 1164, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": 0, "audio_tokens": 0, "reasoning_tokens": 28, "rejected_prediction_tokens": 0}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": 0, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "cached_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_tokens": 0}}, "formatted_response": null, "obfuscation": "Ds44xm8cy3bVtc", "content": "DEFORMATION refers to the mechanical or plastic processing of steel, particularly through hot rolling and controlled rolling. It may be applied in the austenite (γ) region, the γ non-recrystallization region, the γ–α two-phase region, or the ferrite (α) region, including temperatures just below the Aᵣ3 transformation temperature. In rolling schedules, roughing generally occurs in the recrystallization or partial-recrystallization region, whereas finishing commonly takes place in the non-recrystallization region; deformation may also occur in recovery conditions or during stage-three ferrite rolling.\n\nThe temperature, strain, rolling sequence, and state of austenite recrystallization determine the effects of DEFORMATION. When recrystallization is allowed, deformation contr